            <div xml:id="d699">

                <pb facs="0806" n="694" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg694"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13247: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-01T09:00:00+08:00">November 1, 1935—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>110. Embassy’s 99, October 28, 4 p.m. Following telegram has been sent to the
                    Consul General at Hankow by the Embassy: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“November 1, 9 a.m. Reverend C. F. Snyder, Christian and Missionary
                            Alliance, who arrived in Peiping on October 28 with the missionary party
                            from Lanchow, reports that the mission’s station at Minhsien, Kansu, has
                            been occupied since the departure of the missionaries by Central
                            Government troops and that the mission’s station at Taochow, Kansu, has
                            been occupied by Provincial troops. He further reports that the
                            mission’s church at Lintao, Kansu, has probably also been occupied by
                            troops.</p>
                        <pb facs="0807" n="695" xml:id="pg695"/>
                        <p>Please telegraph Chairman of the Kansu Provincial Government and request
                            that the troops in occupation of these American mission stations be
                            ordered to remove from the premises which will be cared for by the
                            mission’s Chinese staff.</p>
                        <p>Counselor Peck at Nanking has been requested to make appropriate
                            representations to the Foreign Office.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
